  "slug": "read-long-ee29",
  "summary": "Archive-scale and multi-study integration now lets transcriptomic datasets serve as reusable reference frameworks for disease and evolutionary comparison.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is transcriptomic data reorganized into reusable reference frameworks. The Human Pancreas Cell Atlas integrates 815,126 single-cell or single-nucleus transcriptomes from 109 donors across 12 studies, defining 94 healthy cell types as a benchmark for disease contextualization. RNAquarium applies archive-scale integration to all public zebrafish SRA RNA-seq data, jointly profiling host and non-host metatranscriptomes to link viral associations with transcriptomic states. The canine gastric cancer atlas combines multi-modal and spatial profiling to map cellular organization, while the cavefish brain atlas identifies 35 cell types and cave-associated regulatory shifts across them. What is new is integration at archive or multi-study scale, with the reference itself serving as the deliverable.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-06-25"
}
